          <a href="/projects/dsh-codebase-memory-mcp" class="nav-next">Next Study: Code Graph →</a>
        </div>
      </nav>

      <!-- Hero Header -->
      <header class="hero">
        <div class="eyebrow-container">
          <span class="badge-flagship">FLAGSHIP CASE STUDY</span>
          <span class="badge-version">v0.7.0 Production</span>
          <span class="badge-tests">1,220+ Passing Tests</span>
        </div>
        <h1>Veyra: Engineering Intelligence for Coding Agents</h1>
        <p class="subtitle">
          Solving the AI coding-agent trust crisis with a Dual-Brain epistemic architecture that rigorously separates repository truth from learned engineering memory.
        </p>

        <div class="meta-row">
          <div class="meta-item">
            <span class="meta-label">Domain</span>
            <span class="meta-val">AI Epistemics & Agent Memory</span>
          </div>
          <div class="meta-item">
            <span class="meta-label">Storage Engine</span>
            <span class="meta-val"><code>node:sqlite</code> + FTS5</span>
          </div>
          <div class="meta-item">
            <span class="meta-label">Integration</span>
            <span class="meta-val">DeepSeek Harness Native Plugin</span>
          </div>
          <div class="meta-item">
            <span class="meta-label">Dependencies</span>
            <span class="meta-val">Zero npm dependencies</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="hero-actions">
          <a href="https://github.com/LoveDoLove/Veyra" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="btn btn-primary">
            <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="currentColor">
              <path d="M12 0C5.37 0 0 5.37 0 12c0 5.31 3.435 9.795 8.205 11.385.6.105.825-.255.825-.57 0-.285-.015-1.23-.015-2.235-3.015.555-3.795-.735-4.035-1.41-.135-.345-.72-1.41-1.23-1.695-.42-.225-1.02-.78-.015-.795.945-.015 1.62.87 1.845 1.23 1.08 1.815 2.805 1.305 3.495.99.105-.78.42-1.305.765-1.605-2.67-.3-5.46-1.335-5.46-5.925 0-1.305.465-2.385 1.23-3.225-.12-.3-.54-1.53.12-3.18 0 0 1.005-.315 3.3 1.23.96-.27 1.98-.405 3-.405s2.04.135 3 .405c2.295-1.56 3.3-1.23 3.3-1.23.66 1.65.24 2.88.12 3.18.765.84 1.23 1.905 1.23 3.225 0 4.605-2.805 5.625-5.475 5.925.435.375.81 1.095.81 2.22 0 1.605-.015 2.895-.015 3.3 0 .315.225.69.825.57A12.02 12.02 0 0024 12c0-6.63-5.37-12-12-12z"/>
            </svg>
            GitHub Repository
          </a>
          <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@lovedolove/veyra" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="btn btn-secondary">
            <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="currentColor">
              <path d="M0 7.334v8h6.666v1.332H12v-1.332h12v-8H0zm6.666 6.664H2.666V9.998h4v4zm5.334 0h-2.666V9.998H12v4zm9.334 0h-4v-1.332H20v-2.668h-4V9.998h5.334v4z"/>
            </svg>
            npm Package
          </a>
        </div>
      </header>

      <!-- Section: The Problem -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>The Problem: The Coding-Agent Trust Crisis</h2>
        <p>
          Coding agents face a fundamental dilemma between <strong>amnesia</strong> and <strong>hallucination</strong>. Without persistent memory, an agent repeats past mistakes, forgets architecture constraints, and rediscovers bugs across restarts. But existing agent memory systems introduce a worse failure mode: they treat remembered observations as authoritative truth.
        </p>

        <div class="callout warning-callout">
          <div class="callout-icon">⚠️</div>
          <div class="callout-body">
            <strong>The Stale Memory Trap:</strong> If an agent stores <em>"file X has function Y"</em> in memory, and a human engineer renames or deletes <em>Y</em>, a naive agent trusts its memory over the actual codebase. It writes patches against code that no longer exists, failing builds or hallucinating non-existent APIs.
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>
          General-purpose memory solutions (e.g. naive vector databases over conversational turns) fail because they treat all text equally. In real-world software engineering, code and engineering context possess strict hierarchical authority:
        </p>
        <ul class="principles">
          <li><strong>Repository Code is Supreme Truth:</strong> What exists on disk right now overrides any past memory, note, or guess.</li>
          <li><strong>Memory is Supporting Context, Never Truth:</strong> Memories are hypotheses, historical rationale, and negative lessons — never hard law.</li>
          <li><strong>Candidate ≠ Truth:</strong> Automatic observations are unverified candidates until backed by reproducible evidence.</li>
          <li><strong>Similarity ≠ Authority:</strong> Vector or lexical search hits prove relevance, not accuracy or correctness.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- Section: Architecture -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Architecture: Dual-Brain & Truth Separation</h2>
        <p>
          Veyra implements a <strong>Dual-Brain</strong> architecture that creates an uncompromised barrier between current repository state and durable engineering memory:
        </p>

        <div class="diagram-box">
          <div class="diagram-header">
            <span class="dot red"></span>
            <span class="dot yellow"></span>
            <span class="dot green"></span>
            <span class="diagram-title">Veyra Dual-Brain Topology</span>
          </div>
          <pre><code>+-------------------------------------------------------------+
|                     DeepSeek Harness Agent                  |
+-------------------------------------------------------------+
         |                                           |
         v (Authoritative: git, tests, AST)          v (Supporting: hybrid search)
+-----------------------------------+     +-----------------------------------+
|            LEFT BRAIN             |     |            RIGHT BRAIN            |
|       Repository Truth Engine     |     |      Durable Memory Engine        |
|                                   |     |                                   |
| - Source code on disk             |     | - Architecture decisions & ADRs   |
| - Git history, diffs, blame       |     | - Verified fix patterns & remedies|
| - Test suites & compiler checks   |     | - Project-specific constraints    |
| - AST code graph (via MCP)        |     | - Negative lessons (known fails)  |
|                                   |     | - Epistemic status: STALE / FRESH |
+-----------------------------------+     +-----------------------------------+
         |                                           |
         +---------------------+---------------------+
                               |
                               v
               +-------------------------------+
               |    Epistemic Arbiter          |
               |                               |
               | Memory contradicts code?      |
               | -> Code wins every time.      |
               | -> Memory flagged as STALE.   |
               +-------------------------------+</code></pre>
        </div>

        <h3>Three Independent Epistemic Axes</h3>
        <p>
          Instead of flattening memory state into a single score, Veyra models durable knowledge across three orthogonal axes, separating persistent storage standing from dynamic read-time validation:
        </p>
        <div class="ladder-grid">
          <div class="ladder-step">
            <span class="ladder-tag candidate">1. Authority Axis</span>
            <p><code>candidate → derived → canonical</code>. Tracks governance: from unverified automatic observation, to verified engineering pattern, up to explicit user-promoted project mandate.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="ladder-step">
            <span class="ladder-tag derived">2. Validation Axis</span>
            <p><code>unverified → reviewed → verified</code>. Tracks empirical confirmation: whether the memory was observed, reviewed, or validated through passing tests.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="ladder-step">
            <span class="ladder-tag canonical">3. Confidence Axis</span>
            <p><code>low / medium / high</code>. Records the certainty assigned by the observer or causal attribution engine.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="ladder-step">
            <span class="ladder-tag stale">Dynamic Read-Time Trust</span>
            <p><code>trusted</code>, <code>review_required</code>, <code>stale</code>, <code>contradicted</code>, <code>insufficient_evidence</code>. Evaluated on-the-fly against live git state and evidence anchors.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section: Key Capabilities -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Core Capabilities</h2>

        <div class="features-grid">
          <div class="feature-card">
            <div class="feature-title">
              <span class="feature-icon">🔍</span>
              <h3>Proactive Recall</h3>
            </div>
            <p>
              Agents automatically search memory at the beginning of non-trivial engineering tasks (bug fixes, refactors, architecture decisions) rather than relying on noisy keyword injections.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="feature-card">
            <div class="feature-title">
              <span class="feature-icon">⚡</span>
              <h3>Evidence-Backed Anchors</h3>
            </div>
            <p>
              Memories anchor directly to file paths, function symbols, line ranges, or commit hashes. If a target file is modified or removed, Veyra marks the memory <code>STALE</code> automatically.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="feature-card">
            <div class="feature-title">
              <span class="feature-icon">🛡️</span>
              <h3>Self-Healing Maintenance</h3>
            </div>
            <p>
              Autonomous health scans flag new contradictions, identify unresolved open questions, detect repeated failure loops, and suggest revalidation candidates without human intervention.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="feature-card">
            <div class="feature-title">
              <span class="feature-icon">🌐</span>
              <h3>Cross-Project Reusability</h3>
            </div>
            <p>
              Strict isolation keeps repo-specific secrets and conventions locked to a project, while project-agnostic patterns (e.g. Node 22 ESM nuances) can be safely promoted to reusable scope.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section: Real-World Engineering -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Engineering Invariants & Reality: What Breaks & How Veyra Handles It</h2>
        <p>
          Building persistent systems for non-deterministic AI agents reveals tricky failure modes. Here are four real challenges solved in Veyra:
        </p>

        <div class="scenarios-list">
          <div class="scenario-item">
            <h4>1. FTS5 Tokenization & Stopword Pitfalls</h4>
            <p>
              Standard SQLite FTS5 tokenizers treat hyphens and punctuation as delimiters, causing identifier searches like <code>dsh-codebase-memory-mcp</code> to match every word independently. Veyra implements token sanitization, quoted phrase fallback, and BM25 ranking calibration tailored for code symbols.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="scenario-item">
            <h4>2. Zero-Dependency Storage Guarantee</h4>
            <p>
              Rather than pulling in heavy native bindings or external services (e.g., PostgreSQL, Redis, Chromadb), Veyra runs purely on Node.js 22's built-in <code>node:sqlite</code> module. This guarantees zero compilation overhead, zero supply-chain vulnerabilities, and instant local execution.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="scenario-item">
            <h4>3. Safe Contradiction Visibility</h4>
            <p>
              Veyra never silently overwrites or auto-deletes conflicting memories. If Memory A claims <em>"use X"</em> and Memory B claims <em>"do not use X"</em>, both records are surfaced to the agent with contradiction flags, while directing the agent to inspect the authoritative repository files to break the tie.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="scenario-item">
            <h4>4. Negative Lessons ([NEGATIVE · known failed solution])</h4>
            <p>
              In software engineering, knowing what <em>didn't work</em> is as valuable as knowing what did. Veyra records falsified hypotheses and failed bug-fix attempts with negative markers, preventing subsequent agent sessions from retrying disproven fixes.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section: Specifications & Verification -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Technical Specifications & Verification</h2>

        <div class="table-container">
          <table class="specs-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Attribute</th>
                <th>Specification / Value</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>Current Release</td>
                <td><span class="mono-badge">v0.7.0</span></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Status</td>
                <td>Active production development on <code>0.7.x</code></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Repository</td>
                <td><a href="https://github.com/LoveDoLove/Veyra" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">github.com/LoveDoLove/Veyra</a></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Package Registry</td>
                <td><a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@lovedolove/veyra" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">@lovedolove/veyra on npm</a></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>DeepSeek Harness Compatibility</td>
                <td><code>&gt;=0.2.0 &lt;0.3.0</code></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Node.js Runtime Bounds</td>
                <td><code>&gt;=22.12.0 &lt;25.0.0</code> (Built with native <code>node:sqlite</code>)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Storage Engine</td>
                <td><code>node:sqlite</code> + FTS5 full-text indexing, isolated per-project DB</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Test Suite Verification</td>
                <td><strong>1,220+ passing tests</strong> (1,221 passing across 19 suites on Node 22 & 24 CI)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Architecture Type</td>
                <td>Dual-Brain Epistemic State Machine</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>License</td>
                <td>MIT License</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </section>

      <Footer />
    </article>
  </body>
</html>

<style>
  :root {
    --bg: #080b12;
    --card-bg: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.02);
    --card-border: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.06);
    --text: #f1f5f9;
    --muted: #94a3b8;
    --dim: #64748b;
    --accent: #6366f1;
    --accent-light: #818cf8;
    --accent-cyan: #06b6d4;
    --accent-emerald: #10b981;
    --accent-amber: #f59e0b;
    --font-mono: "JetBrains Mono", ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    --radius: 12px;
  }

  body {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    background-color: var(--bg);
    color: var(--text);
    font-family: "Inter", -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI", Roboto, sans-serif;
    line-height: 1.6;
    background-image: 
      radial-gradient(circle at 50% 0%, rgba(99, 102, 241, 0.08) 0%, transparent 40%),
      linear-gradient(rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.015) 1px, transparent 1px),
      linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.015) 1px, transparent 1px);
    background-size: 100% 100%, 48px 48px, 48px 48px;
    background-position: 0 0, -1px -1px, -1px -1px;
    min-height: 100vh;
    overflow-x: hidden;
  }

  /* Ambient Glows */
  .glow-bg {
    position: absolute;
    pointer-events: none;
    z-index: 0;
  }

  .top-glow {
    top: -100px;
    left: 50%;
    transform: translateX(-50%);
    width: 600px;
    height: 350px;
    background: radial-gradient(circle, rgba(99, 102, 241, 0.15) 0%, rgba(6, 182, 212, 0.05) 60%, transparent 80%);
  }

  .side-glow {
    top: 600px;
    right: -150px;
    width: 500px;
    height: 500px;
    background: radial-gradient(circle, rgba(99, 102, 241, 0.08) 0%, transparent 70%);
  }

  .case-study {
    max-width: 840px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 32px 20px 80px;
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
  }

  /* Navigation */
  .nav-bar {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    align-items: center;
    padding-bottom: 24px;
    margin-bottom: 32px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--card-border);
  }

  .back-link, .nav-next {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
    color: var(--muted);
    text-decoration: none;
    font-size: 13.5px;
    font-weight: 500;
    transition: color 0.15s ease;
  }

  .back-link:hover, .nav-next:hover {
    color: var(--accent-light);
  }

  /* Hero Section */
  .hero {
    margin-bottom: 48px;
  }

  .eyebrow-container {
    display: flex;
    gap: 8px;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    margin-bottom: 16px;
  }

  .badge-flagship {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 700;
    background: rgba(99, 102, 241, 0.18);
    color: var(--accent-light);
    border: 1px solid rgba(99, 102, 241, 0.3);
    padding: 3px 10px;
    border-radius: 4px;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
  }

  .badge-version {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 600;
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.05);
    color: var(--muted);
    border: 1px solid var(--card-border);
    padding: 3px 10px;
    border-radius: 4px;
  }

  .badge-tests {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 600;
    background: rgba(16, 185, 129, 0.15);
    color: var(--accent-emerald);
    border: 1px solid rgba(16, 185, 129, 0.25);
    padding: 3px 10px;
    border-radius: 4px;
  }

  .hero h1 {
    font-size: 38px;
    font-weight: 800;
    line-height: 1.2;
    margin: 0 0 16px;
    letter-spacing: -0.025em;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .subtitle {
    font-size: 17px;
    color: var(--muted);
    line-height: 1.6;
    margin: 0 0 28px;
  }

  .meta-row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);
    gap: 12px;
    padding: 16px;
    background: var(--card-bg);
    border: 1px solid var(--card-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    margin-bottom: 24px;
  }

  .meta-item {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 4px;
  }

  .meta-label {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 10px;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--dim);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
  }

  .meta-val {
    font-size: 12.5px;
    color: var(--text);
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  .meta-val code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 11px;
    color: var(--accent-cyan);
    background: rgba(6, 182, 212, 0.1);
    padding: 2px 4px;
    border-radius: 3px;
  }

  .hero-actions {
    display: flex;
